{# gfadmin/templates/admin/items.html -- the OrderItem list.

   Its own template rather than admin/list.njk + renderList(): Django's page
   labels its button "New Item" (not list.njk's generic "New") and has no
   CSV/filter controls at all, so it fits none of list.njk's variable bits
   and would have meant bending the shared template for one caller. The
   pagination block and table classes below are copied from list.njk so the
   two pages still look identical.

   The row count in the h1, the "None" row and the pagination nav are the
   port's standard list-page additions -- see admin/list.njk, whose
   `{{ title }} ({{ total }})` shape this deliberately mirrors, and
   slug_redirects.njk:7-9, which says the same. Django's heading is a bare
   `<h2>Items</h2>` (items.html:8) with no count, because its table is
   unpaginated.

   Django's items.html `{% load humanize %}` is loaded but NEVER applied --
   neither cell goes through |intcomma -- so calories renders raw here too.
   The third <th> is deliberately empty: it heads the Edit-button column
   (items.html:19). #}
{% extends "admin/page.njk" %}

{% block title %}Items{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="columns">
  <div class="column">
    <h1 class="title">Items ({{ total }})</h1>

    <div class="buttons">
      <a href="/admin/item/new/" class="button is-link is-light">New Item</a>
    </div>

    <table class="table is-fullwidth is-hoverable">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          {# Sort links are new: Django's headers are plain text (items.html:
             16-20), which was fine for one unpaginated table and is not once
             the list is 12 pages long. #}
          <th>
            <a href="?sort={{ name_sort }}">Name</a>{% if sort_field == "name" %}{% if direction == "desc" %} &darr;{% else %} &uarr;{% endif %}{% endif %}
          </th>
          <th>
            <a href="?sort={{ calories_sort }}">Calories</a>{% if sort_field == "calories" %}{% if direction == "desc" %} &darr;{% else %} &uarr;{% endif %}{% endif %}
          </th>
          <th></th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {% for item in items %}
        <tr>
          <td>{{ item.name }}</td>
          <td>{{ item.calories }}</td>
          <td><a href="{{ item.edit_url }}" class="button is-small is-light">Edit</a></td>
        </tr>
        {% else %}
        <tr><td colspan="3">None</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <nav class="pagination" role="navigation" aria-label="pagination">
      {% if page > 1 %}<a class="pagination-previous" href="?page={{ page - 1 }}&amp;sort={{ sort }}">Previous</a>{% endif %}
      {% if has_next %}<a class="pagination-next" href="?page={{ page + 1 }}&amp;sort={{ sort }}">Next page</a>{% endif %}
      <span class="pagination-list">Page {{ page }} of {{ total_pages }}</span>
    </nav>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
